// Getting started: a provision script that configures a guest on `up` and on demand.
page gs_first_provision {
  title = "Automate a guest with a script"
  chapter_header "Automate a guest with a script" {
    kicker       = "Getting started · tutorial"
    reading_time = "9 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    A provision script is a [wscript](guide_scripting) file declared inside
    the machine it configures. vmlab runs it during `vmlab up` once that
    machine is ready. In this tutorial you write one that waits for the guest,
    runs a command, copies a file in and captures the screen, then run the
    same script again on demand with `vmlab script`.
  DOC

  h2 "Before you start"

  list {
    li "You have completed [Your first VM](gs_first_vm), or have any lab with one Linux VM that reports ready. The lab file below is that tutorial's, extended."
    li "The guest has a display. Screenshots need one, and every VM has one. A container does not, so the screenshot step would fail there by naming the missing capability."
  }

  h2 "Start from a real one"

  p <<DOC
    The `alpine-registry` example ships a provision that does the first two
    things this tutorial needs: it waits for readiness and runs a command.
    Read it before writing your own.
  DOC

  code rust {
    filename    = "examples/alpine-registry/scripts/setup.ws"
    source_file = "../../examples/alpine-registry/scripts/setup.ws"
    dedent      = true
  }

  p "Four things in this file are the shape of every provision script."

  list {
    li "`use vmlab` imports the host module, which provides `vmlab::sleep_ms` and the other helpers in [wscript API: Term, types and the vmlab module](ref_ws_types)."
    li "`fn main(lab: Lab)` is the entry point. vmlab calls it with the [Lab handle](ref_ws_lab), from which the script reaches every machine and segment by name."
    li "Methods that can fail return `Result`. The `?` operator propagates an error string out of `setup`, and `expect` in `main` turns it into a failed provision, which fails `vmlab up` with that message."
    li "`wait_ready(600)` blocks until the guest agent answers or 600 seconds pass. A provision runs after its machine is ready, so the call is instant during `up`, but it makes the script correct under `vmlab script` too, where nothing has waited for you."
  }

  h2 "Write the script"

  p <<DOC
    In the directory holding the `first-vm` lab file, create `scripts/setup.ws`
    and a file to copy in, `scripts/files/motd`.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
mkdir -p scripts/files
printf 'provisioned by vmlab\n' > scripts/files/motd
SRC
  }

  code rust { filename = "scripts/setup.ws" source = <<'SRC'
// Wait for the guest, run a command, copy a file in, capture the screen.

use vmlab

fn setup(lab: Lab) -> Result[unit, string] {
    let alp = lab.vm("alp")?

    alp.wait_ready(600)?
    lab.log("alp is ready at " + alp.ip()?)

    // exec: program and argument list, captured stdout/stderr/exit code.
    let rel = alp.exec("/bin/cat", ["/etc/alpine-release"])?
    if rel.exit_code != 0 {
        return Err("could not read the release: " + rel.stderr)
    }
    lab.log("alpine release: " + rel.stdout.trim())

    // copy_to: a host path relative to this script, to an absolute guest path.
    alp.copy_to("files/motd", "/etc/motd")?
    let motd = alp.exec("/bin/cat", ["/etc/motd"])?
    lab.log("guest motd: " + motd.stdout.trim())

    // screenshot: a PNG under the lab's .vmlab/screenshots/ when the path is "".
    let shot = alp.screenshot("")?
    lab.log("screen captured to " + shot)
    Ok(())
}

fn main(lab: Lab) {
    setup(lab).expect("first-vm setup failed")
}
SRC
  }

  p "Three details are easy to get wrong."

  list {
    li "`exec` takes a program and a list of arguments, not a shell line. To run a pipeline or use a shell feature, call the shell: `alp.exec(\"/bin/sh\", [\"-c\", \"...\"])`. The default timeout is 120 seconds; `exec_timeout` takes a third argument in seconds."
    li "Relative host paths in `copy_to`, `copy_from`, `screenshot` and the image-matching methods resolve against the directory the script lives in, not the lab root. That is why the file is at `scripts/files/motd` and the script names it as `files/motd`."
    li "`screenshot(\"\")` picks a timestamped name under the lab-local `.vmlab/screenshots/` directory and returns the path. Pass a path to choose the file yourself."
  }

  h2 "Declare it on the machine"

  p "Add a `provision {}` block to the VM. The path is relative to the lab root."

  code wcl { filename = "vmlab.wcl" source = <<'SRC'
import <vmlab.wcl>

lab "first-vm" {

  vm "alp" {
    template = "ghcr.io/vmlabdev/vmlab-templates/alpine-3.23"
    arch     = "x86_64"
    memory   = 1GiB
    nic { nat = true }

    provision "scripts/setup.ws" { }
  }
}
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    `validate` compiles the script as part of validating the lab file, so a
    syntax error or a call to a method that does not exist is reported before
    anything boots.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab validate" }

  callout "The script compiles" {
    kind = :success
    body = "`vmlab validate` reports the lab as ok with one VM. A typo in the script is reported here, with its line."
  }

  h2 "Run it with up"

  code sh { source = "vmlab up" }

  p <<DOC
    `up` boots the VM, waits until it is ready, and then runs the provision.
    Each `lab.log` line appears in the terminal as the script reaches it.
    Every `up` runs the steps again, whether or not the VM was already
    running, so keep scripts safe to repeat. Copying the same file twice is harmless. Creating an account twice
    is not, and the [mixed-lab](apx_examples) example shows the guard pattern,
    checking the guest's state before changing it.
  DOC

  p "Confirm the two side effects from the host."

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab exec alp -- cat /etc/motd
ls .vmlab/screenshots/
SRC
  }

  callout "The guest is provisioned" {
    kind = :success
    body = "`/etc/motd` in the guest reads `provisioned by vmlab`, and `.vmlab/screenshots/` holds a PNG named after the VM and the time."
  }

  h2 "Run it again on demand"

  p <<DOC
    Any script can be run ad hoc against the running lab. `vmlab script`
    takes a path relative to the lab root and calls its `main` with the same
    lab handle a provision gets.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab script scripts/setup.ws" }

  p <<DOC
    The one difference is ownership. A provision belongs to the machine that
    declared it, and `lab.this_vm()` returns that machine. Under `vmlab
    script` there is no owning machine and `this_vm()` returns an error. The
    script above uses `lab.vm(\"alp\")` so it works both ways.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Provisions and playbooks are the two kinds of setup step, and they run
    interleaved in declaration order. The other place scripts run is an
    `on {}` handler, which reacts to an event such as `vm.crashed`. See
    [Playbooks](guide_playbooks) and [Events and handlers](guide_events).
  DOC

  callout "Provisions run as the agent identity" {
    kind = :note
    body = "With no `login {}` on the machine, `exec` and `copy_to` run as root or SYSTEM. To write into a user's home as that user, declare a login and take a second handle with `as_login`. [Attach an editor to a dev machine](gs_dev_attach) uses it, and [Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins) explains the rules."
  }

  h2 "Next steps"

  list {
    li "[Guest automation with wscript](guide_scripting) covers the execution model, error handling, the terminal API and multi-machine orchestration."
    li "[Screens, input and vision](guide_vision) shows `wait_for_text`, `wait_for_image`, keystrokes and mouse input, for guests where the agent is not there yet."
    li "[wscript API: Machine](ref_ws_machine) lists every method on the machine handle, and [vmlab script](cmd_script) documents the verb."
  }
}
